"novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-cosmological-moral-neutrality","pro-cosmological-transcendent-personal-cause"],"explanation":"It extends the scope objection by connecting moral neutrality directly to the cumulative evidential balance."}},{"id":"ai-con-premise-underdetermined-morality","text":"Objective morality, if it exists, does not by itself select theism unless divine grounding outperforms non-theistic realism and avoids making goodness depend circularly on the nature of the deity whose goodness is disputed.","novelty":{"classification":"repairs","sourceMoveIds":["con-moral-argument-premises-and-counterevidence","pro-theistic-moral-explanation"],"explanation":"It repairs the live critique by supplying a substantive rival-grounding burden instead of relying chiefly on cited disagreement."}},{"id":"ai-con-premise-historical-baseline","text":"Resurrection reports warrant divine intervention only if their detailed evidential profile is markedly less expected under ordinary testimonial, legendary, psychological, and social mechanisms considered jointly than under resurrection.","novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-resurrection-testimony-base-rate","pro-resurrection-explanatory-context"],"explanation":"It extends the base-rate objection by identifying the combined ordinary mechanisms requiring explicit likelihood comparison."}}],"conclusion":{"id":"ai-con-conclusion-withhold-christian-theism","text":"Therefore, absent a demonstrated asymmetry in moral grounding, providential prediction, or resurrection likelihood, the cumulative evidence warrants withholding belief in the perfectly good Christian God and treating suffering as substantial contrary evidence.","novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-historical-suffering-evidence","con-parity-of-observational-exclusion","con-resurrection-testimony-base-rate"],"explanation":"It extends the live conclusion while proportionately recommending withholding belief rather than claiming deductive disproof."}},"newArguments":[{"id":"ai-con-new-predictive-specificity","title":"Predictive specificity test","text":"Unknown-purpose defenses become evidentially costly when they protect a hypothesis from every possible observation. A good-God account should specify, at least approximately, why some distributions of suffering are more expected than others: for example, why nonhuman suffering predates human agency, why harms fall so unevenly, or why many victims receive no visible developmental benefit. Without risky expectations, providential explanations accommodate the data after the fact but do not predict them. The evil-creator mirror exposes this weakness because equally flexible unknown goods can immunize the opposite moral hypothesis against abundant goodness.","novelty":{"classification":"introduces","sourceMoveIds":[],"explanation":"This introduces a new predictive-specificity criterion for comparing flexible providential explanations."}},{"id":"ai-con-new-evidential-dependence","title":"Dependence-adjusted resurrection evidence","text":"Several resurrection data points may not provide independent support if they descend from overlapping traditions, shared communities, or later interpretation of the same experiences. A rigorous comparison should model those dependencies before multiplying the force of the empty tomb, appearances, and transformed belief. It should also compare resurrection with combinations of ordinary causes rather than demand one natural explanation reproduce every detail. This approach grants that the reports can remain historically striking while preventing correlated testimony from being counted repeatedly as though each item independently overcame the low baseline probability of bodily resurrection.","novelty":{"classification":"introduces","sourceMoveIds":[],"explanation":"This introduces a new dependence-adjustment argument for evaluating the combined historical evidence."}}]}}}
